{
  "item_id": "7f93d63faaf11f733276a443",
  "version_id": "1c8d31d1a52853a33abbf82a2909b64babf8ea03d6714eb54e2df4ed7128e44a",
  "synopsis": "IdeaScientist contributes a developer-authored evaluation of research proposal generation, with particular value for pieces 02 and 03: specifying inspectable experiment plans and scrutinizing the evidence used to judge them. Its agents produce proposals with baselines, ablations and falsification conditions; the evaluated systems stop before implementation. Its evidentiary role is an attributed methodological case study and proposal-level benchmark, not evidence of successful autonomous experimentation or independent replication. The supplied metadata dates publication to October 2, within the frozen September 16–October 15 window; October 9 is an update. These orientation notes do not replace passage citations.",
  "limitations_and_counterevidence": "The roughly 15K target papers include training data; only 277 are held-out tests. Table 1's 74.6 versus 60.6 supports a 14.0-point difference, whereas the source describes a 14.0% advantage. Preserve that numeric ambiguity rather than repeating it as a relative improvement. E.1 says the test split was used only for final evaluation, while F.4 compares selected and superseded checkpoint combinations on the full test split; selection chronology remains unresolved. Do not use these results to establish superiority or cost savings. Human comparisons did not show significant advantages over DeepScientist or IdeaScientist-Base. Judge checks are the authors' own validation, including their reported negligible self-preference; human review remains a safeguard, not evidence that bias occurred. External systems did not receive identical budget caps. Output-token counts omit total monetary cost and do not establish end-to-end efficiency; substantial training hardware is disclosed. The source also acknowledges residual model-memory contamination. Code and dataset links are supplied, but their contents and independent replication are not established by this extraction. Training uses LoRA weight updates, not merely revised instructions. All later substantive claims require their own validated passage.",
  "passages": [
    {
      "purpose": "A.2: Operational contribution to experiment planning—require comparison methods, ablations, falsifiable predictions and explicit limitations, while distinguishing plans from measured outcomes.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "| Proposed evaluation | The evaluation setting, benchmarks, baselines, metrics, and ablations the method would use, stated as a plan without measured results. | \n| Falsifiable predictions | Empirical claims the method implies, each paired with the observation that would falsify it. | \n| Limitations | Three to five concise limitations, acknowledged or apparent from the method. | "
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "§4.2: Establish the actual training/validation/test denominators and explain how human papers become model-generated, results-masked reference proposals; qualify the abstract's roughly 15K framing.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "These filters yielded \\(14{,}977\\) target papers. We randomly partitioned them into \\(14{,}500\\) training papers, \\(200\\) validation papers, and \\(277\\) held-out test papers, and used the same partition for all three agents. Qwen3.6-27B converts each human-authored target paper into the required proposal schema, preserving the research problem, proposed method, model design, related work, and limitations, while removing all measured experimental outcomes. This results-masked representation records directions that human researchers have pursued without exposing their experimental success."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "§5.1: Define the controlled comparison and its decisive boundary: shared inputs and retrieval tools, native external loops, and no implementation or experimentation.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "For a fair comparison, all systems receive the same problem definition and output schema and use the same tools to access our offline paper database for literature retrieval, while the target paper remains hidden and internet access is disabled. External AutoResearch systems and general-purpose agents retain their native agent loops. For end-to-end research systems, we instruct the agent to stop once a machine learning research proposal is complete rather than continuing to implementation or experimentation."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Table 1, Qwen3.6-27B rows: Preserve reported baseline, harness and trained results. Columns are All/Cutoff/In-domain/average novelty; relevance/clarity/specificity/actionability/soundness/impact/average proposal quality; overall. The overall difference 74.6−60.6 is 14.0 points; specificity also decreases from Base to Trained.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "| DeepScientist | 57.6 | 58.5 | 15.2 | 43.8 | 94.0 | 85.8 | 77.8 | 74.9 | 61.9 | 70.6 | 77.5 | 60.6 | \n| IdeaScientist-Base | 66.0 | 67.1 | 67.5 | 66.9 | 96.2 | 89.9 | 80.6 | 77.3 | 62.8 | 73.4 | 80.0 | 73.5 | \n| IdeaScientist-Trained | 67.3 | 68.3 | 70.9 | 68.8 | 98.2 | 90.3 | 77.9 | 77.8 | 64.5 | 73.5 | 80.4 | 74.6 | "
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "§5.2: Human assessment supplies supporting evidence and important counterevidence: a small blinded sample and nonsignificant comparisons with DeepScientist and Base.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "We ran a blind pairwise human evaluation on 50 randomly selected papers from the held-out test split. For each paper, two annotators compared the proposal from IdeaScientist-Trained with proposals from other systems on the same backbone, including IdeaScientist-Base. System names were hidden. Figure [2](#S5.F2)(b) reports the win rates: IdeaScientist-Trained beats every other state-of-the-art system by a statistically significant margin, with DeepScientist the only exception. IdeaScientist-Trained also wins more often than IdeaScientist-Base, although the difference is not statistically significant."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "§5.4: Preserve the authors' judge-validation argument alongside human disagreement and their counterevidence to a same-family bias allegation. These are internal checks, not independent confirmation of scientific value.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "First, scoring research proposals is inherently subjective, and even the two experts disagree to a notable degree (\\(r=0.56\\)), much as reviewers do in conference peer review. Second, each LLM judge correlates with the human average at a level comparable to the agreement between the two annotators (\\(r=0.51\\)–\\(0.59\\) for top-3 judges). LLM judges are therefore a reasonable proxy for expert assessment.\nFigure [4](#S5.F4)(b–c) further measures two properties of each judge: its agreement with the cross-judge consensus, and its self-preference bias toward generation systems from its own model family. Qwen3.6-27B agrees strongly with the consensus, second only to the frontier Claude-4.8 judge, and the difference between the two is not statistically significant. Its self-preference bias is negligible and lower than Claude-4.8’s, justifying why we chose it as the main judge."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "E.1: Preserve the claimed validation-only checkpoint selection and final-test boundary; read together with F.4 before drawing performance conclusions.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "For each sub-agent, we select the checkpoint with the highest held-out validation reward; the test split is used only for final evaluation. The reported system uses gap-finder step 380, innovator step 310, and report-writer step 194."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "E.1: Document unmatched external budget caps and the training infrastructure relevant to student feasibility and cost accounting; hardware allocation is not a monetary cost estimate.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "The open source autoresearch systems and the general purpose agents run their own native agent loops, and we do not impose these caps on them. Each role is trained on \\(40\\) nodes of eight H100 GPUs, with \\(32\\) nodes dedicated to policy optimization and \\(8\\) nodes to asynchronous rollout generation."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "F.4: Expose the checkpoint-selection reproducibility gap by preserving the full-test comparison of multiple combinations. This requires chronology clarification; it does not alone establish test leakage.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "We trained each role over multiple checkpoints and evaluated more than one checkpoint combination. Table [12](#A6.T12) compares the selected combination (gap finder step 380, innovator step 310, and report writer step 194) with the superseded combination (steps 122, 116, and 80) on the full test split under the same judge and metrics."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "G.1: Preserve the authors' output-token comparison with adjacent qualifications about endogenous compute and early failures. Use as a reported resource measure, not proof of lower total cost or superior efficiency.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "On the same Qwen3.6-27B backbone and harness, IdeaScientist-Trained uses \\(168{,}916\\) output tokens per delivered proposal, \\(11.1\\%\\) fewer than the \\(189{,}942\\) used by IdeaScientist-Base, so its gains do not result from a larger generation budget. Within the fixed per-role turn and token caps, the per-run generation budget still varies across papers. That variation is endogenous: harder papers can drive more turns while scoring lower, whereas runs that fail the validity gate early spend less and score zero."
      }
    }
  ]
}
